One Thread Per Client

In the thread-per-client model, every new client connection gets its own dedicated thread.

Client A → Thread 1
Client B → Thread 2
Client C → Thread 3
...

Each thread is responsible for handling that client's entire TCP connection, including all requests sent over a Keep-Alive connection.

Why is it simple?
Each client has its own thread.
No client blocks another client.
Easy to understand and implement.
Every thread maintains its own stack and execution state.
Why is it inefficient?

Suppose 10,000 clients connect.

10000 Clients

↓

10000 Threads

Each thread requires:

A stack.
Thread metadata.
OS scheduling information.

Most clients are not continuously sending requests. They may simply keep the connection open and remain idle.

This means thousands of threads may exist just waiting for network activity.

Context Switching

Suppose the machine has only 8 CPU cores.

Although there are 10,000 threads, only 8 threads can execute simultaneously.

The operating system keeps switching between threads.

