The producer accepts incoming client connections and places them into a queue.

The worker threads continuously wait for new jobs from the queue.

Why is it better?

Suppose 10,000 clients connect.

Instead of creating:

10000 Threads

the server creates only:

8 Threads

These threads are reused throughout the lifetime of the server.

Memory usage becomes:

8 Threads

↓

8 Stacks

instead of

10000 Threads

↓

10000 Stacks
Context Switching

Since only a small number of worker threads exist, the operating system only needs to schedule those workers.

This greatly reduces context-switching overhead.

Do jobs still wait?

Yes.

Waiting does not disappear.

The difference is where the waiting happens.

